;;; A wildcard `_` in a rule's `:match` matches nothing — M1e2 **W20**,
;;; register row `W20/wild-in-match` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1e2.2.20 (2026-11-05) from S1e2.2.18's parked note, ruled by the user on
;;; 2026-11-04 (Q-M1e2.13 (a)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a)`, and one rule that concludes `(hit w)` from any
;;; `holds` fact: its pattern's one argument is the wildcard `_`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `_` in a rule's pattern matches any value (`01_grammar.md`'s token table,
;;; *head / arg wildcard in patterns*, and the user's ruling), so `w` fires on
;;; `(holds a)` and root holds `(hit w)`. The blind enumerator proposes
;;; `(hit a)`, so R is two states, `{(hit w)}` and `{(hit a), (hit w)}`. It
;;; is the answer of `wild-in-match-twin.ein`, this file with `_` written as
;;; a variable the rule reads nowhere else.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.20 `w` never fired: the rule compiler lowered `_` as an
;;; opaque slot, which unifies with nothing (`compile.rs`' `slot`). Root held
;;; no `hit`, and R was `∅` and `{(hit a)}`, no state holding `(hit w)` (`ein
;;; solve FILE` at `c73a0796`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle`
;;; the same). Since then `_` is a slot that matches any value and binds
;;; nothing, and the program answers its twin's R.

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a)
(rule w () :match (holds _) :assert (hit w))
